# The `bsalons` project — the same database the Flutter app and the operator
# console use. This app is a client of it; it owns no schema.
#
# One pair of credentials serves both halves of the merged application: the
# marketing pages read the salon index at BUILD time to prerender it, and the
# waitlist form calls `join_app_waitlist()` from the browser; the product reads
# and writes as its signed-in user through `@supabase/ssr`.
#
# The publishable (anon) key is safe in a browser: Row-Level Security is the
# gate, and every write goes through a SECURITY DEFINER RPC that authorises the
# caller itself. The waitlist is the sharpest illustration — `app_waitlist` has
# RLS on with no policies and no table grants, so this key cannot read one row
# of the list it just joined.
#
# There is no service-role key here and there must never be one. This app acts
# only as its signed-in user — that is the whole security model.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# The public origin, used for canonical URLs, `metadataBase`, the sitemap and
# robots. Read through `lib/site.ts`.
#
# `||`, not `??`, in that module: a declared-but-blank value is a common CI
# accident and would otherwise produce `https:///salon/…`.
#
# Inlined at `next build` because it is `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, so changing it needs a
# rebuild rather than a redeploy of the same bundle.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=

# The operator console (`../admin`), a separate application on its own origin.
# Only the marketing footer's "Admin portal" link would read it.
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL` used to live here too and is **gone**: it pointed at
# `tho_web` as a different origin, and the merge made the product these same
# routes. An unset value would have sent a production visitor to their own
# machine. Links into the product are relative now — see `brand.appPaths`.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ADMIN_URL=
# Google Search Console's HTML-tag verification token — the `content` value only,
# not the whole `<meta>` tag.
#
# Deliberately NOT `NEXT_PUBLIC_`: `app/layout.tsx` is a server component and
# `metadata` resolves on the server, so the token reaches the document head
# without entering a client bundle. Unset means the tag is absent entirely.
#
# Read at `next build` like everything else here, so set it on the host before
# the build you expect to carry it. Verifying by DNS TXT instead needs no value
# here at all — and gives a Domain property, which covers the apex and `www`
# together. That is the recommended route; this is the escape hatch.
GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# GA4 measurement id (`G-XXXXXXXXXX`). Unset renders no `<GoogleAnalytics>`, so
# no script, no cookie and no request to Google — the right default for
# `next dev` and for previews.
#
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_` is correct here, unlike the token above: the id travels in the
# `gtag.js` URL, so the browser needs it and there is nothing to withhold.
#
# Client-side navigations are counted by GA4's own Enhanced measurement ("page
# changes based on browser history events"), not by this app. Leave that on in
# the data stream or only the first page of each visit is recorded.
#
# **Before setting this in production, the privacy policy has to change.**
# `lib/marketing/content.ts` names two processors (Supabase and Firebase) and
# lists no analytics collection; GA4 is a third, and it sets cookies. That text
# is linked from the App Store and Play declarations, so a gap here contradicts
# something already filed.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_MEASUREMENT_ID=

# Facebook app id, rendered as `fb:app_id`. **Optional, and nothing is broken
# without it.**
#
# Meta's Sharing Debugger reports it as a "required property" on a page whose
# preview it has just rendered correctly — which is the proof that it is not.
# The property fed Facebook Domain Insights, which Meta shut down on
# 2021-06-30. It matters now only for Facebook Login, the Share dialog's app
# attribution, and Messenger extensions, none of which this site uses.
#
# Set it only if a Meta app exists for its own reasons. Unset renders no tag.
NEXT_PUBLIC_FACEBOOK_APP_ID=
